IAM:
AWS IAM (Identity and Access Management)
IAM = Identity and Access Management.
The AWS root user has complete access to the AWS account and can perform almost any action. Because giving everyone such high-level access is not secure and can lead to accidental changes or misuse, AWS provides IAM to control who can access which AWS resources and what actions they are allowed to perform.
Why do we use IAM?
IAM helps us implement controlled and secure access to AWS resources.
With IAM, we can:
•	Create users for individuals who need AWS access.
•	Create groups and add users with similar responsibilities to those groups.
•	Create roles that provide a specific set of permissions.
•	Create policies that define exactly what actions are allowed or denied.
•	Give users or roles specific permissions instead of giving unnecessary full access.
•	Follow the Principle of Least Privilege — give only the permissions required to perform the job.
•	Control access to services such as EC2, S3, RDS, Lambda, etc.
Example
Suppose an organization has:
•	Developer → needs access to EC2 and application resources.
•	Database Administrator → needs access to RDS.
•	Developer team → several developers need similar permissions.
Instead of giving everyone full AWS access, we can:
Create users → Add users to groups → Attach appropriate policies → Give only required permissions.
For example:
Developers Group → EC2 permissions
DBA Group → RDS permissions
This reduces the possibility of unauthorized access, accidental changes, and misuse of resources.
Important IAM Components
1. User
Represents an individual or application identity that needs AWS access.
2. Group
A collection of IAM users. We can attach permissions to the group so that all users in that group receive the required permissions.
3. Policy
A JSON document that defines what actions are Allowed or Denied on which AWS resources.
Example:
Allow → Read → S3 bucket
4. Role
A role provides permissions that can be assumed by trusted entities, such as AWS services, users, or applications. Roles are commonly used when an application or AWS service needs temporary access without storing long-term access keys.
Root User vs IAM User
Root User:
•	Created when the AWS account is created.
•	Has extremely broad/full account permissions.
•	Should not be used for normal daily activities.
•	Should be protected with MFA.
IAM User/Role:
•	Used for normal access and specific tasks.
•	Permissions can be controlled using IAM policies.
•	Helps implement least-privilege access.
One important interview point
IAM is mainly about authentication and authorization.
•	Authentication → Who are you?
•	Authorization → What are you allowed to do?
Easy way to remember
IAM = Who can access AWS + What they can access + What actions they can perform.
The main goal of IAM is secure, controlled, least-privilege access to AWS resources.

 

AWS IAM & AWS Organizations
1. IAM Permission Policies
•	Policies define what actions a user, group, or role can perform on AWS resources.
•	IAM policies are written in JSON format.
•	We can use AWS managed policies or create our own customer-managed policies.
•	We should follow the Principle of Least Privilege — give only the permissions required.
Example:
A user may be allowed to:
EC2 → Start/Stop instances
but not:
EC2 → Delete instances
2. IAM Groups
•	A group contains multiple IAM users with similar permissions.
•	We can attach policies to the group so that users in that group receive those permissions.
•	Users can be added to a group when the group is created or later.
•	This makes permission management easier.
Example:
Developers Group → EC2 + S3 permissions
3. AdministratorAccess vs Root
•	AdministratorAccess provides very broad permissions through an IAM policy.
•	It is similar to having administrative access, but it is not exactly the same as the AWS root user.
•	The root user has the highest level of account authority and has some account-level actions that an IAM administrator cannot perform.
•	Root should generally be used only for root-specific account tasks and protected with MFA.
4. IAM Role
•	An IAM role provides permissions that can be assumed by a trusted entity.
•	Roles are commonly attached to AWS resources such as EC2 instances, allowing applications running on those instances to access other AWS services without storing access keys.
Example:
EC2 → IAM Role → S3 permissions
The application running on EC2 can then access the required S3 resources.
Important: A role does not automatically allow an EC2 server to connect to another server. The role provides AWS API permissions; network connectivity and the target service's permissions are separate.
________________________________________
AWS Organizations
AWS Organizations helps centrally manage multiple AWS accounts.
We can create separate accounts for different teams, departments, environments, or projects.
Example:
Production Account
Development Account
Testing Account
Finance Account
This provides better isolation, security, billing, and centralized management between accounts.
SCP – Service Control Policy
•	SCPs are used with AWS Organizations to set the maximum permissions available to accounts or organizational units (OUs).
•	An SCP does not grant permissions by itself.
•	It acts as a guardrail and can restrict what an account can do.
Example:
If an organization wants an account to operate only in the Mumbai (ap-south-1) region, an SCP can deny actions in other AWS regions.
So even if an IAM user has permission to create an EC2 instance, the SCP can prevent that action outside the allowed region.
Easy Difference to Remember
IAM Policy:
👉 What can this user/role do?
SCP:
👉 What is this AWS account allowed to do at maximum?
IAM Role:
👉 What permissions can a trusted user/service/resource assume?
AWS Organizations:
👉 How do we centrally manage multiple AWS accounts?
Interview Memory Line
IAM = Identity & permissions
Role = Temporary/assumable permissions
Policy = JSON-based permission rules
Group = Collection of users
Organizations = Manage multiple AWS accounts
SCP = Guardrail/maximum permission boundary for accounts or OUs

 

