EC2 : 
AWS EC2 – Elastic Compute Cloud
EC2 = Elastic Compute Cloud
Amazon EC2 is an AWS service that provides virtual servers (compute instances) in the cloud.
Instead of purchasing and maintaining a physical server, we can create an EC2 instance and use it on-demand.
Basic EC2 Flow
Choose AMI → Choose Instance Type → Configure Key Pair → Configure Security Group → Launch EC2
________________________________________
1. AMI – Amazon Machine Image
AMI = Amazon Machine Image
An AMI is a template used to create an EC2 instance.
It contains things such as:
•	Operating system
•	Required software/configuration
•	Root volume information
Example:
Ubuntu AMI → Launch EC2 → Get an Ubuntu server.
We can use AWS-provided AMIs or create our own custom AMI.
________________________________________
2. Instance Type
Instance type determines the resources available to the EC2 server, such as:
•	CPU
•	Memory (RAM)
•	Network performance
•	Storage capabilities
Example:
t3.micro → small workload
Different instance families are designed for different workloads.
________________________________________
3. Key Pair
A key pair is used to securely authenticate to an EC2 instance.
It contains:
•	Public key → stored on the EC2 instance.
•	Private key → kept by the user.
For Linux EC2 instances, the private key is commonly used with SSH to connect without entering a normal password.
Example:
Laptop → SSH + Private Key → EC2 Linux Server
Important: Never share or lose the private key.
For Windows EC2 instances, key pairs are commonly used to obtain/decrypt the initial Administrator password.
________________________________________
4. Security Group
A Security Group acts as a virtual firewall for an EC2 instance.
It controls:
•	Inbound traffic → traffic coming into the EC2 instance.
•	Outbound traffic → traffic going out from the EC2 instance.
Security groups are stateful, meaning return traffic for an allowed connection is automatically allowed.
Common Ports
Purpose	Protocol	Port
SSH	TCP	22
HTTP	TCP	80
HTTPS	TCP	443
RDP	TCP	3389
For example:
SSH enabled → Port 22 → allows SSH connection to Linux EC2
HTTP enabled → Port 80 → allows web traffic
HTTPS enabled → Port 443 → allows secure web traffic
Security Best Practice
Do not unnecessarily allow:
SSH → 0.0.0.0/0
This means SSH is open to the entire internet.
Instead, restrict SSH access to a specific trusted IP/range where possible.
________________________________________
5. Public IP / Private IP
An EC2 instance can have:
•	Private IP → communication within the VPC/network.
•	Public IPv4 address → allows internet communication when properly configured.
•	Elastic IP → static public IPv4 address that can be associated with an instance/resource.
________________________________________
6. Storage
EC2 commonly uses EBS (Elastic Block Store) for persistent block storage.
Example:
EC2 → EBS Volume → OS/Data
________________________________________
7. Important EC2 Terms
AMI → Template used to launch an EC2 instance.
Instance Type → Defines CPU, memory, and other compute characteristics.
Key Pair → Used for secure authentication.
Security Group → Controls inbound and outbound traffic.
EBS → Persistent block storage for EC2.
VPC → Virtual network in which the EC2 instance runs.
Easy Interview Memory
EC2 = Virtual server in AWS
AMI = Server template
Instance Type = CPU/RAM capacity
Key Pair = Secure login/authentication
Security Group = Virtual firewall
Port 22 = SSH
Port 80 = HTTP
Port 443 = HTTPS
EBS = Persistent storage
VPC = Network for EC2

Absolutely. Your basic understanding is correct, but a few points need correction—especially private IP, public IP, Elastic IP, and EBS. Here is a clean interview-ready version.
AWS EC2 – IP Addresses & EBS Notes
1. Public IP Address
•	A public IP address is used for communication between an EC2 instance and the internet.
•	It is not fixed by default.
•	If you stop and start an EC2 instance, the public IP can change.
•	If you only reboot the instance, the public IP normally remains the same.
•	A public IP is automatically assigned when the instance is launched if the subnet/instance configuration allows it.
Interview answer: 
"A public IP is used for communication between an EC2 instance and the internet. By default, it is not a permanent IP and can change when the instance is stopped and started."
________________________________________
2. Private IP Address
•	A private IP address is used for communication inside the VPC/private network.
•	It can be used for communication between:
o	EC2 → EC2
o	EC2 → RDS
o	EC2 → other AWS resources
•	The primary private IP of an EC2 instance generally remains associated with the instance throughout its lifecycle.
•	Private IPs are not directly reachable from the public internet.
⚠️ Correction to your note:
Don't say "Private IP is always static." A private IP can be dynamically assigned, but the primary private IP remains with the EC2 instance until the instance is terminated.
Interview answer: 
"A private IP is mainly used for internal communication within a VPC. It is not directly accessible from the internet."
________________________________________
3. Elastic IP Address (EIP)
•	An Elastic IP is a static public IPv4 address provided by AWS.
•	It remains associated with your AWS account until you release it.
•	You can associate it with an EC2 instance.
•	If the EC2 instance is stopped and started, the Elastic IP remains the same when it is still associated.
•	It is useful when you need a fixed public IP, for example:
o	Public-facing application
o	Whitelisted IP
o	Server that needs a fixed address
Important: AWS now charges for public IPv4 addresses, including Elastic IPs, so don't simply say "Elastic IP is chargeable only when used." Say:
"Elastic IP/public IPv4 addresses are chargeable under AWS's current public IPv4 pricing."
________________________________________
4. EBS – Elastic Block Store
EBS = Elastic Block Store
Think of EBS as a virtual hard disk for an EC2 instance.
It provides persistent block-level storage that can be attached to an EC2 instance.
Why do we use EBS?
For storing:
•	Operating system
•	Application files
•	Database files
•	Logs
•	Data files
For example:
EC2 Instance
     |
     |---- Root EBS Volume → Operating System
     |
     |---- Additional EBS Volume → Application/Data
Important EBS points
•	EBS is block storage.
•	An EBS volume exists independently of the EC2 instance's compute resources.
•	You can attach additional EBS volumes to an EC2 instance.
•	You can increase the size of an EBS volume later.
•	You can take EBS snapshots for backup/recovery.
•	EBS volumes are normally associated with a specific Availability Zone.
•	EBS provides persistent storage, so data can remain even when an EC2 instance is stopped.
________________________________________
5. EBS Volume Types
One important type is General Purpose SSD.
GP2
•	Older/general-purpose SSD volume type.
•	Performance is linked to volume size.
•	IOPS are automatically calculated based on the volume size.
GP3
•	Newer and generally preferred for new workloads.
•	You can independently configure:
o	Volume size
o	IOPS
o	Throughput
•	You don't necessarily need to increase the disk size just to get more IOPS.
Simple interview difference:
gp2: Performance/IOPS is tied more closely to volume size.
gp3: Storage size, IOPS and throughput can be configured more independently.
________________________________________
6. Creating Additional EBS Volumes
Yes, you can create additional volumes later.
For example:
EC2
 |
 |--- Root Volume
 |      100 GB
 |
 |--- EBS Volume 1
 |      200 GB → Application
 |
 |--- EBS Volume 2
        500 GB → Database
Typical process:
1.	Create an EBS volume.
2.	Select the required size/type.
3.	Select the same Availability Zone as the EC2 instance.
4.	Attach the volume to the EC2 instance.
5.	Inside the operating system, identify the new disk.
6.	Format it if required.
7.	Mount it to a directory.
8.	Start using it for application/data storage.
