AWS EBS (Elastic Block Store)
EBS = Elastic Block Store
EBS provides persistent block storage for EC2 instances. It works like a hard disk attached to an EC2 server.
1. EBS Volume Size
•	EBS volume size can generally be increased.
•	You cannot directly decrease/shrink an existing EBS volume.
•	If you need a smaller volume, typically:
1.	Create a snapshot.
2.	Create a new smaller volume from the required data.
3.	Attach the new volume to the server.
•	EBS volumes are persistent, so data remains even if the EC2 instance is stopped or restarted.
2. One EBS Volume → Multiple Servers
•	Normally, one EBS volume is attached to one EC2 instance at a time.
•	However, EBS Multi-Attach allows certain EBS volume types, such as io1/io2, to be attached to multiple compatible EC2 instances simultaneously.
•	Multi-Attach has specific limitations and is not the normal use case.
Interview point:
Don't say "one EBS volume can always be attached to multiple servers." Say EBS Multi-Attach supports multiple EC2 instances for supported volume types and configurations.
3. Detach and Attach EBS
An EBS volume can be:
EC2 Server A → Detach → EC2 Server B → Attach
Important:
•	The EC2 instances should generally be in the same Availability Zone as the EBS volume.
•	EBS volumes are AZ-specific, not directly attachable across AZs.
•	To use the data in another AZ, you can create a snapshot and create a new EBS volume in the required AZ.
4. EBS Snapshot
A snapshot is a point-in-time backup of an EBS volume.
Example:
EBS Volume → Snapshot → New EBS Volume
Important points:
•	Snapshot is stored in Amazon S3-managed storage by AWS.
•	You can use a snapshot to create a new EBS volume.
•	The new volume can then be attached to another EC2 instance.
•	Snapshots are incremental after the first snapshot, so only changed blocks need to be stored.
•	Snapshots can be used for backup, restore, migration, and creating copies of volumes.
•	You can copy snapshots to another Region and create an EBS volume there.
Important Correction
A snapshot is not an EC2 instance backup in the same sense as a full server image.
•	EBS Snapshot → Backup of an EBS volume.
•	AMI (Amazon Machine Image) → Used to create new EC2 instances with an OS/configuration and attached volume information.
So remember:
EBS Snapshot = Volume backup
AMI = EC2 image/template
________________________________________
EC2 User Data
EC2 User Data allows us to automatically run commands/scripts when an EC2 instance is launched.
It is commonly used for bootstrapping a server.
Why do we use User Data?
Suppose we launch a new Linux EC2 server and want to automatically:
•	Install Apache/Nginx
•	Install Docker
•	Install required packages
•	Create directories
•	Start services
•	Download application files
•	Perform initial server configuration
Instead of doing these manually, we can put commands in User Data.
Example
#!/bin/bash

yum update -y
yum install -y httpd
systemctl start httpd
systemctl enable httpd
When the EC2 instance starts, these commands can automatically configure the server.
Important Points
•	User Data is mainly used for initial instance configuration / bootstrapping.
•	It can contain shell scripts on Linux.
•	Windows EC2 instances can use PowerShell/Windows commands.
•	User Data is provided when launching the EC2 instance.
•	It is useful for automation and reducing manual configuration.
•	User Data is not a replacement for configuration-management/IaC tools such as Terraform, Ansible, etc.
•	Be careful about putting passwords, API keys, or secrets directly in User Data because it is not a good place for sensitive information.
Easy Interview Definition
EC2 User Data is a script or set of commands that can be provided during EC2 launch to automatically bootstrap and configure the instance.
