Load balancer : 
   AWS Load Balancer (ELB)
What is a Load Balancer?
A Load Balancer distributes incoming client traffic across multiple servers/EC2 instances.
Why do we use a Load Balancer?
When one server receives too much traffic, it can become:
•	Overloaded
•	Slow
•	Unresponsive
•	A single point of failure
Instead of sending all traffic to one server, the Load Balancer distributes the traffic among multiple healthy servers.
Simple Example
Without Load Balancer:
Users → Server 1
If Server 1 becomes overloaded or fails → Application becomes unavailable
With Load Balancer:
Users → Load Balancer → Server 1
** → Server 2**
** → Server 3**
If Server 1 fails, the Load Balancer can stop sending traffic to Server 1 and send traffic to the healthy servers.
________________________________________
Types of AWS Load Balancers
AWS provides different types of Load Balancers:
1. Application Load Balancer (ALB)
•	Works at OSI Layer 7 – Application Layer.
•	Used mainly for HTTP and HTTPS traffic.
•	Can make routing decisions based on:
o	Hostname
o	URL path
o	HTTP headers
o	Query parameters
•	Commonly used for web applications and microservices.
Example:
example.com/orders → Orders service
example.com/products → Products service
2. Network Load Balancer (NLB)
•	Works primarily at OSI Layer 4 – Transport Layer.
•	Supports TCP, UDP and TLS traffic.
•	Designed for very high performance and low latency.
•	Useful when applications need high network throughput and connection performance.
3. Gateway Load Balancer (GWLB)
•	Works at OSI Layer 3 – Network Layer.
•	Used mainly to deploy and scale network/security appliances.
•	Examples:
o	Firewalls
o	Intrusion detection/prevention systems
o	Deep packet inspection appliances
________________________________________
OSI Layer – Easy Remembering
Load Balancer	OSI Layer	Common Use
ALB	Layer 7	HTTP/HTTPS web applications
NLB	Layer 4	TCP/UDP/TLS, high performance
GWLB	Layer 3	Security/network appliances
Easy Interview Trick
ALB → Application → Layer 7
NLB → Network traffic → Layer 4
GWLB → Gateway/network → Layer 3
________________________________________
Target Group
A Target Group is a logical group of backend resources where the Load Balancer sends traffic.
Targets can include:
•	EC2 instances
•	IP addresses
•	Lambda functions (for ALB)
Important Point
The client does not normally directly communicate with the backend server when using a Load Balancer.
The flow is:
Client → Load Balancer → Target Group → Healthy Target
For example:
User
↓
ALB
↓
Target Group
↓
EC2-1 / EC2-2 / EC2-3
The Target Group helps the Load Balancer determine which backend targets should receive traffic.
________________________________________
Health Checks
The Load Balancer performs health checks on targets.
Example:
ALB
 ↓
Target Group
 ↓
EC2-1 → Healthy ✅
EC2-2 → Healthy ✅
EC2-3 → Unhealthy ❌
The Load Balancer sends traffic only to the healthy targets.
If EC2-3 becomes healthy again, it can be added back into traffic distribution.
________________________________________
Load Balancer and High Availability
Load Balancers help achieve:
•	High availability
•	Fault tolerance
•	Scalability
•	Traffic distribution
•	Reduced impact of server failure
For production applications, we generally place backend servers across multiple Availability Zones.
Example:
                    Load Balancer
                         |
              -----------------------
              |                     |
             AZ-1                  AZ-2
              |                     |
           EC2-1                 EC2-2
           EC2-3                 EC2-4
If one server or even one Availability Zone has a problem, traffic can be directed to healthy targets.
________________________________________
⭐ Interview Answer
Question: Why do we use a Load Balancer?
A Load Balancer distributes incoming traffic across multiple backend servers. It prevents a single server from becoming overloaded and improves application availability and fault tolerance. It also performs health checks and avoids sending traffic to unhealthy targets.
Question: What is a Target Group?
A Target Group is a logical group of backend targets such as EC2 instances, IP addresses, or Lambda functions. The Load Balancer uses the Target Group to route traffic to healthy targets.
Question: What is the difference between ALB and NLB?
ALB works at Layer 7 and is mainly used for HTTP/HTTPS traffic with application-level routing. NLB works at Layer 4 and is designed for high-performance TCP, UDP, and TLS traffic.
⭐ Remember This Flow
Client → Load Balancer → Target Group → Healthy EC2
And:
ALB = L7
NLB = L4
GWLB = L3


AWS Auto Scaling
What is Auto Scaling?
Auto Scaling automatically increases or decreases the number of EC2 instances based on application demand.
It helps the application handle increased traffic while avoiding unnecessary infrastructure costs when traffic is low.
Simple Example
Suppose our application normally needs 2 servers:
Normal Traffic
      ↓
Auto Scaling Group
      ↓
EC2-1 + EC2-2
If traffic increases:
High Traffic
      ↓
Auto Scaling Group
      ↓
EC2-1 + EC2-2 + EC2-3 + EC2-4
When traffic decreases, unnecessary instances can be terminated:
Low Traffic
      ↓
Auto Scaling Group
      ↓
EC2-1 + EC2-2
This is called scaling out and scaling in.
________________________________________
Auto Scaling Group (ASG)
An Auto Scaling Group (ASG) manages a collection of EC2 instances.
We define important settings such as:
1. Minimum Capacity
The minimum number of EC2 instances that should normally be running.
Example:
Minimum = 2
The ASG tries to maintain at least 2 instances.
2. Desired Capacity
The normal/desired number of EC2 instances we want running.
Example:
Desired = 2
3. Maximum Capacity
The maximum number of EC2 instances that the ASG is allowed to launch.
Example:
Maximum = 5
So:
Minimum  = 2
Desired  = 2
Maximum  = 5
If traffic increases, ASG can increase:
2 → 3 → 4 → 5
But it will not go beyond the configured maximum of 5.
________________________________________
Scaling Out and Scaling In
Scale Out
When demand/traffic increases, Auto Scaling adds EC2 instances.
2 EC2
  ↓
High traffic
  ↓
3 EC2
  ↓
4 EC2
Scale Out = Add servers
Scale In
When demand decreases, Auto Scaling removes EC2 instances.
5 EC2
  ↓
Low traffic
  ↓
3 EC2
  ↓
2 EC2
Scale In = Remove servers
________________________________________
Fault Tolerance
Auto Scaling improves fault tolerance.
For example:
ASG
 |
 ├── EC2-1 ✅
 ├── EC2-2 ✅
 └── EC2-3 ❌
If an EC2 instance becomes unhealthy or fails, the Auto Scaling Group can launch a replacement instance to maintain the required capacity.
For better availability, EC2 instances can be distributed across multiple Availability Zones.
________________________________________
Cost Management
Auto Scaling can help control costs.
High traffic
More instances are launched:
2 → 4 → 5 EC2
This provides enough capacity for users.
Low traffic
Unnecessary instances are removed:
5 → 3 → 2 EC2
This prevents us from paying for more EC2 capacity than we need.
So Auto Scaling helps balance:
Performance + Availability + Cost
________________________________________
Auto Scaling + Load Balancer
Auto Scaling is commonly used together with a Load Balancer.
                    Users
                      ↓
               Load Balancer
                      ↓
              Target Group
                      ↓
        -------------------------
        ↓           ↓           ↓
      EC2-1       EC2-2       EC2-3
        ↑           ↑           ↑
        └──── Auto Scaling ─────┘
The Load Balancer distributes traffic, while the Auto Scaling Group manages the number of EC2 instances.
Easy Difference
Load Balancer → Distributes traffic
Auto Scaling → Adjusts number of servers
________________________________________
⭐ Interview Answer
What is Auto Scaling?
AWS Auto Scaling automatically adjusts the number of EC2 instances based on application demand. It can launch additional instances when traffic increases and terminate unnecessary instances when traffic decreases. This helps improve availability, fault tolerance, scalability, and cost efficiency.
⭐ Remember
Minimum → Lowest number of instances
Desired → Normal/target number of instances
Maximum → Highest number of instances
Scale Out → Add EC2 instances
Scale In → Remove EC2 instances
Load Balancer → Distributes traffic
Auto Scaling → Changes the number of servers
 
